{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":0,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, √22","reason":"Let X be the second intersection of line BD with the circle. Then BX=AB²/BC=12 and DX=12-6=6. The chord product at D gives r²-OD²=DC·DX=18, so r²=22 and r=√22.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1976_AMC_12_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"tangent-secant-and-chords","label":"Tangent-secant and chord products","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-secant-and-chords":["math:power-of-a-point"]},"nodeIds":["math:power-of-a-point"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_055","cnm_147","cnm_074"],"id":"tangent-secant-and-chord-products","label":"Use tangent and chord products","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-secant-and-chord-products":["math:power-of-a-point"]},"nodeIds":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_250","cnm_055","cnm_147","cnm_074"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 10","reason":"Let PB = t > 0, so PC = t + 20. The tangent-secant relation gives t(t + 20) = (10√3)² = 300. Thus (t - 10)(t + 30) = 0, and the positive length is t = 10.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1956_AMC_12_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_055","cnm_168"],"id":"tangent-secant-product","label":"Use the tangent-secant relation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-secant-product":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_224","cnm_250","cnm_055","cnm_168"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 30","reason":"Similarity gives AD*AE=11*20=220. Power at A gives AB*AF=220, so F is beyond B with AF=20. Also cos(A)=(11^2+20^2-24^2)/(2*11*20)=-1/8. Hence CF^2=20^2+20^2-2*20*20*(-1/8)=900, so CF=30.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_12B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"secant-products-and-similarity","label":"Use secant products and similarity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"secant-products-and-similarity":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_058","cnm_253","cnm_271","cnm_272","math:general-cosine-law-application"]},"nodeIds":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_058","cnm_253","cnm_271","cnm_272","math:general-cosine-law-application"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 73","reason":"PA=18 and PC(PC+7)=8·18=144, giving PC=9. The cosine rule gives AC²=243, so PA²=PC²+AC² and AC is perpendicular to CD. Thus AD is a diameter, and r²=(243+49)/4=73.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1992_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_224","cnm_116"],"id":"secants-and-diameter","label":"Find a right angle and a diameter","methodDirectNodeIds":{"secants-and-diameter":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_140","cnm_254","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_140","cnm_254","cnm_174","cnm_250","cnm_224","cnm_116"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 17","reason":"Put d=BD. Supplementary angles A and C give (73-d^2)/48=-(40-d^2)/24, hence d^2=51. Similarity gives XF/XC=XY/XD=(3/4-11/36)/(1/4)=16/9. Intersecting chords give XC·XG=BX·XD=3d^2/16. Therefore XF·XG=(16/9)(3·51/16)=17.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_12A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"similarity-power-cosines","label":"Similarity, chord products, and the diagonal","methodDirectNodeIds":{"similarity-power-cosines":["cnm_271","cnm_272","math:power-of-a-point","cnm_255","cnm_h064","math:general-cosine-law-application"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_271","cnm_272","math:power-of-a-point","cnm_255","cnm_h064","math:general-cosine-law-application"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 2√22","reason":"AC=16. Power at A gives AH(AH+7)=2·15=30, so AH=3, BJ=6, and BH=13. Put x=BD and y=BE. Powers at B and C give xy=78 and (16-x)(16-y)=14, hence x+y=20. Thus DE²=(y-x)²=20²-4·78=88, so DE=2√22.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1982_AMC_12_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_139","cnm_055","cnm_250","cnm_146","cnm_169"],"id":"secant-products","label":"Compare secant products at the vertices","methodDirectNodeIds":{"secant-products":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_224","cnm_148"]},"nodeIds":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_224","cnm_148","cnm_139","cnm_055","cnm_250","cnm_146","cnm_169"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 5","reason":"Extend BP beyond P to Q with PQ=3. Then DB=1 and DQ=5. Isosceles triangle APQ gives ∠AQD=∠BCD, and vertical angles give triangle DAQ similar to triangle DBC. Thus AD·CD=DB·DQ=5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1997_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"circumcircle-power","label":"Circle power","methodDirectNodeIds":{"circumcircle-power":["cnm_253","math:power-of-a-point"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_253","math:power-of-a-point"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_053","cnm_055","cnm_062","cnm_117","cnm_250"],"id":"auxiliary-circle","label":"Recognize an intersecting-chord product","methodDirectNodeIds":{"auxiliary-circle":["cnm_137","cnm_115","math:power-of-a-point"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_137","cnm_115","math:power-of-a-point","cnm_053","cnm_055","cnm_062","cnm_117","cnm_250"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 3","reason":"The altitude from C meets AB at its midpoint M, with MB=1 and CM²=49−1=48. Then MD²=64−48=16, so BD=4−1=3. Equivalently, DB·DA=8²−7²=15.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2005_AMC_12B_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"circle-power","label":"Use a circle centered at C","methodDirectNodeIds":{"circle-power":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_224"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 61","reason":"Set L = BC and d = CX. Power of C gives Ld = 97^2-86^2 = 2013 = 3·11·61. Also 0<d<L and 11<L<183. The only admissible factor pair is d = 33 and L = 61, giving BX = 28.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_12A_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"secant-power","label":"Use power of point C","methodDirectNodeIds":{"secant-power":["math:power-of-a-point"]},"nodeIds":["math:power-of-a-point"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: AP²=PB·AB","reason":"Let r=OB and d=AD=AP. Then AB=2r, AO=d+r, and (d+r)²=(2r)²+r². Hence d²=4r²-2rd=AB(AB-AP)=AB·PB, proving A.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1960_AMC_12_Q32","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"tangent-secant-product","label":"Use tangent-secant power","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-secant-product":["cnm_261","math:power-of-a-point"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_261","math:power-of-a-point"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: √65","reason":"Take E=(0,0), A=(-2,0), B=(6,0), and D=(0,-3). The perpendicular bisector of AB places the center at (2,h). Equal radii to A and D give 16+h²=4+(h+3)², so h=1/2. The diameter is 2√(16+1/4)=√65.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1972_AMC_12_Q32","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"intersecting-chord-products","label":"Chord products and midpoints","methodDirectNodeIds":{"intersecting-chord-products":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_251","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_251","cnm_174"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_055","cnm_074"],"id":"chord-products-and-midpoints","label":"Chord products and chord midpoints","methodDirectNodeIds":{"chord-products-and-midpoints":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_251","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_251","cnm_174","cnm_250","cnm_055","cnm_074"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 13 cm","reason":"With radius r, the half-chord is 12 and its distance from the center is r - 8. Thus r² = 12² + (r - 8)², giving 16r = 208 and r = 13.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1987_AMC_12_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"intersecting-chord-products","label":"Use intersecting chord products","methodDirectNodeIds":{"intersecting-chord-products":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 24π","reason":"DF = 6 + 2 = 8. Because CD is a diameter, angle DFC is 90°. Triangles DOE and DFC are similar, so r/6 = 8/(2r). Hence r² = 24 and the circle's area is 24π.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1995_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"chord-product-pythagoras","label":"Chord products and Pythagoras","methodDirectNodeIds":{"chord-product-pythagoras":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_174"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 61","reason":"Because C is outside the circle, the point order is C, X, B. Thus CX*CB=97^2-86^2=2013=3*11*61. Both factors are positive integers, CX<CB, and triangle inequalities require 11<CB<183. Of the factor pairs (1,2013), (3,671), (11,183), and (33,61), only the last is feasible. Therefore BC=61.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_10A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"secant-power-product","label":"Power of a point","methodDirectNodeIds":{"secant-power-product":["math:power-of-a-point"]},"nodeIds":["math:power-of-a-point"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: AO·AB","reason":"Since ∠AMB=∠AOP=90°, triangles AOP and AMB are similar. Thus AP/AB=AO/AM, giving AP·AM=AO·AB.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1957_AMC_12_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"intersecting-chords-and-pythagoras","label":"Chord products and Pythagoras","methodDirectNodeIds":{"intersecting-chords-and-pythagoras":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_174","cnm_055"]},"nodeIds":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_174","cnm_055"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_064","cnm_110"],"id":"chord-products-and-pythagoras","label":"Combine chord products with Pythagoras","methodDirectNodeIds":{"chord-products-and-pythagoras":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_174","cnm_055","cnm_147"]},"nodeIds":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_174","cnm_055","cnm_147","cnm_250","cnm_064","cnm_110"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (64π)","reason":"CB is perpendicular to AD, so AB = 16/2 = 8. Pythagoras gives CB² = 10² - 8² = 36. The annular area is π(100 - 36) = 64π.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_8_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"tangent-secant-power","label":"Tangent-secant power","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-secant-power":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_147"]},"nodeIds":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_147"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 8√6","reason":"Let the outer chord length be L. Its inner portion has length L/2, so the common squared distance to the center is 19²-(L/2)²=17²-(L/4)². Hence 3L²/16=72, yielding L=8√6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_12A_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"secant-products","label":"Power of an outer endpoint","methodDirectNodeIds":{"secant-products":["cnm_251","math:power-of-a-point","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_251","cnm_220"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, two values: t=3 and t=4","reason":"Here t²=m(10-m), with m in {1,…,9} and m≠5. Up to exchanging the arcs, m=1,2,3,4 gives products 9,16,21,24. Only the first two are squares, yielding t=3 or 4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1960_AMC_12_Q35","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h209","cnm_250","cnm_074"],"id":"solution:enumerate-arc-lengths:6e7fd07ddd73","label":"Enumerate the possible arc pairs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"enumerate-arc-lengths":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_075","math:power-of-a-point"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_075","math:power-of-a-point","cnm_h209","cnm_250","cnm_074"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 360 square units","reason":"Set A=(0,0), C=(20,0), and D=(20,30). Since E=(5,0), B=(t,2t-10). The right angle at B gives (t-2)(t-10)=0. Because E lies on segment BD, t=2, so B=(2,-6). The area is 20(30+6)/2=360.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_10A_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"diameter-circle-chord-products","label":"Diameter circle and chord products","methodDirectNodeIds":{"diameter-circle-chord-products":["cnm_254","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_174","math:power-of-a-point","cnm_p150"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_254","cnm_272","cnm_174","math:power-of-a-point","cnm_271","cnm_p150"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 360","reason":"Take A=(0,0), C=(20,0), D=(20,30), and E=(5,0). Since E lies on segment BD, write B=(5−t,−2t), t>0. The right-angle condition at B gives t²+2t−15=0, so t=3 and B has height 6 below AC. The area is 20(30+6)/2=360.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_12A_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"diameter-circle-chord-products","label":"A circle and intersecting chords","methodDirectNodeIds":{"diameter-circle-chord-products":["cnm_254","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_174","math:power-of-a-point","cnm_p150"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_254","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_174","math:power-of-a-point","cnm_p150"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"math:power-of-a-point","status":"mapped","total":20}
